findings.claim:
Dazed's examples: a Number (N)ine hoodie with Undercover 85 jeans; a Hysteric Glamour tee under a pink plaid shirt with beat-up Jeremy Scott trainers. Lekondo (a secondary aesthetics-index site) describes the formula as:
- deep-V tees layered under open shirts;
- archive denim (mud-wash, hand-reworked, patchwork or narrow selvedge), baggy or flared and stacked over sneakers;
- graphic jersey tops, oversized puffers and rock-graphic outerwear;
- long layered silver chains, a low-slung monogram belt and archive designer glasses;
- palette of washed indigo and mud-brown denim on a black/grey base, with red and print pops and silver hardware.
nss adds plain blue jeans, tees, worn-out shoes and jeans falling apart at the seams.

The status brand of the rage/Opium scene. Playboi Carti helped popularise it, and Destroy Lonely calls his Rick-plus-grunge look 'glunge'. Long-running memes:
- outsiders think his sneakers (the 'dunks', debuted FW06) are knock-off Converse (on TikTok since 2021);
- 'full Rick' means head-to-toe Rick, e.g. at Thanksgiving;
- the Rick Owens table.
New Geobaskets retail about $1,165-$1,556 (Lyst, Nov 2026), including mega-bumper and jumbo-lace versions. Some purists say Rick isn't 'archive' because he's a current designer.

Ballhog deliberately mixes in 2010s swag and gaudy 2000s luxury. Examples:
- beat-up Jeremy Scott adidas wing trainers (Dazed);
- 2000s True Religion jeans now listed as a 'grail';
- Famous Stars and Straps tees tagged ballhog;
- monogram belts and archive Chanel/YSL glasses (Lekondo);
- Bape, which Complex says rebounded in 2025 on Y2K nostalgia.

The canon:
- 1990s-2000s Japanese labels (Number (N)ine, Undercover, Hysteric Glamour, If Six Was Nine, L.G.B.) plus CDG, Yohji Yamamoto and Issey Miyake;
- late-90s Helmut Lang, Martin-era Margiela and early-2000s Raf Simons;
- Hedi's Dior Homme (2000-2007), 90s JPG and Vivienne Westwood.
Rick Owens and Chrome Hearts are worn constantly, but some purists call them current rather than archive. The 2023 brands most associated with archive on TikTok were Rick Owens, Raf Simons, Margiela and Westwood. The next status game resellers point to is 'true vintage': clothing 50+ years old, e.g. military surplus.

Vinted's own rules:
- counterfeits are banned (listing removed, account suspended);
- no 'inspired by [brand]' or '[brand]-like' wording;
- no items copying a brand's recognisable shape or pattern;
- no unrelated brand names in the brand field, title, description or hashtags;
- luxury items may need proof such as a receipt.
Grailed's help centre states zero tolerance for replicas (text seen in search results; the page blocked automated reading) and authenticates higher-risk items. Depop's Top Seller code reportedly says to name only brands that made the item (search summary; the PDF was unreadable).

Head-to-toe black with leather and distressing. Proportions are either oversized on top or a fitted top over flared or very wide jeans. Pieces:
- black flared skinny leather pants, leather jackets, long draped tops;
- heavy black boots (Rick Owens is the classic pick) or beat-up sneakers;
- silver chains, cross pendants and stacked rings (Chrome Hearts);
- studded belts and bullet belts;
- beanies, balaclavas or ski masks, wraparound or statement sunglasses;
- black nail polish and dark makeup.
Colours: black first, then washed lavender or bone, a deep red accent, and silver. Fans at shows favour all-black layers, ripped pieces and ski masks. Grazia's only women-specific note is K-pop idols in beanies, Chrome Hearts jewellery and big sunglasses.

Through 2025-2026 indie sleaze overtook Y2K as the trend of the moment. Women's pieces:
- holey tees, tiny tank tops, leopard print;
- skinny and low-rise jeans with studded belts;
- leather jackets, leopard fur coats, military-style jackets, thin scarves;
- dirty ballet flats, pointed boots and wellies;
- smudged, dripping eyeliner.
Critics say the revival feels too polite.

Cues documented in the sources:
- smudged, slightly dripping black liner (indie sleaze);
- black nail polish and dark makeup (Opium);
- matte overlined lips, carved brows and cut crease (2016 revival);
- silver jewellery over gold;
- wigs and costume for visual kei moments;
- for men, bleached or pearly hair with a black ombre (Nettspend).
No source documented piercing norms.

Vinted's IP rules ban copying a brand's recognisable shape, pattern or design, not just its logo. Brand marks seen in this world's sources include:
- the Hysteric Glamour 'Girl' graphic (named in listings);
- the Off-White yellow belt;
- monogram belts (LV);
- Bape camo;
- the Jeremy Scott adidas wing trainer;
- Number (N)ine pieces printed with a real musician's face.
Treat the following as off-limits on screen too: Dior oblique, Gucci GG and web stripe, Chanel CC, YSL, Burberry check, Supreme box logo, Nike swoosh and the panelled Tech Fleece look, adidas stripes and trefoil, the Converse star patch and striped toe cap, Margiela's back-stitch tab and numbered label, the Rick Owens bumper plus oversized tongue plus jumbo-lace combination, Sp5der web, Hellstar star, Von Dutch, the Playboy bunny, band logos, Sanrio and Nana characters, and Marlboro.
